# Changelog

All notable changes to this project are recorded in this file.

The format is based on [Keep a Changelog](https://keepachangelog.com/en/1.1.0/),
and this project adheres to [Semantic Versioning](https://semver.org/spec/v2.0.0.html).

## 2026-10-21 — 1.1.0 (minor)

### Minor Release

This release completes the documentation of the ontology and extends its controlled vocabularies. Every class and property now carries a scope note, an example and a Turtle usage example; literature references are added, and annotation defects are corrected — invalid or broken example snippets, references to undeclared terms, a wrong DCMI `Software` type, and a misfiled annotation predicate that hid the temporal-property examples from the generated docs.

The four concept schemes gain a `skos:notation` on every concept, French labels next to the English ones, and two new digital dimensions, Process Automation and Data Flow; English labels become singular and Title Case, and the definitions of Data, Digital Public Service and Digital Solution are sharpened. The vocabulary shapes now require notations and both languages. The model changes are backwards-compatible additions, hence a minor release. The shapes are stricter: data using category values outside the vocabularies, or a prescriptive rule without a deontic modality (which the ontology already required), now fails validation. The example and pilot data now use only concepts of the vocabularies, which the data shapes check; the vocabulary shapes select concepts by scheme rather than by list.

The generated documentation is reorganised (Abstract, class diagram, Design Overview, Namespaces, grouped Classes), the class diagram is redesigned, the light/dark mode works, every reference in the ontology is listed, and each release from 1.0.0 on is published at its own address.

#### Documentation & References

- Reworked the ontology's front-matter: `dcterms:description` is now a concise one-line summary (the Abstract), and the full narrative — structure, theoretical grounding, reuse strategy — lives in a new `skos:note` rendered as the Design Overview; the earlier `dcterms:abstract` was removed to avoid duplication
- Moved use cases to a dedicated `skos:scopeNote` on the ontology, trimmed to 7 use cases directly supported by the model
- Added `rdfs:seeAlso` reference to Zilli, L., Corazza, M., Palmirani, M., & Sapienza, S. (2025) on `ConstitutiveRule` and `Definition` classes
- Added `rdfs:seeAlso` reference to Governatori, G., & Palmirani, M. (2026) on `ConstitutiveRule` class
- Clarified in `ScopeRule` scope note that scope rules are not definitions
- Converted bibliographic references with DOIs from string literals to IRI objects with `rdfs:label`, improving RDF(S) compliance and enabling clickable links in generated docs:
  - Rawls (1955) → `https://doi.org/10.2307/2182230`
  - Austin (1962) → `https://doi.org/10.1093/acprof:oso/9780198245537.001.0001`
  - Searle (1969) → `https://doi.org/10.1017/CBO9781139173438`
  - Searle (1979) → `https://doi.org/10.1017/CBO9780511609213`
  - Hohfeld (1913) → `https://doi.org/10.2307/785533`
  - Grossi, Meyer, Dignum (2006) → `https://doi.org/10.1093/logcom/exl027`
  - Ceci et al. (2018) → `https://doi.org/10.1007/978-3-030-00178-0_7`
  - Zilli et al. (2025) → `https://doi.org/10.3233/FAIA251593`
  - Governatori & Palmirani (2026) → `https://doi.org/10.1145/3769126.3769257`
- Added the project README (#150): what LOMO is, links to the published documentation, the repository layout, how to validate and build locally, and how to contribute. The 1.0.0 entry recorded a README that was never committed, although `pyproject.toml` points to it. `scripts/README.md` now documents `build_site.py` and the `--status`, `--pages-url` and `--version-path` options of `generate_docs.py`

#### Generated Documentation

- Added example on fishing trips for Temporal Specifications
- Fixed the diagram for better readability
- Made the **Usage Note** opened by default
- Restructured the document flow: a concise Abstract (from `dcterms:description`), then the Ontology Class Diagram, a **Design Overview**, Conformance, the class/property reference, Controlled Vocabularies, and a Revision History
- Added a **Design Overview** section sourced from the graph (a new `skos:note` on the ontology); its subsections are numbered and listed in the table of contents, with **Use Cases** (from `skos:scopeNote`) as the first subsection
- Grouped the Classes section thematically (Rules / Actions & Results / Agents / Temporal) to mirror the diagram filters, replacing the flat alphabetical list; renamed the section heading "Entities" → "Classes"
- Added a **Conformance** section (RFC 2119 keyword boilerplate plus a statement that instance data conforms via the SHACL shapes in `shapes/lomo-shapes.ttl`)
- Surfaced this CHANGELOG in the generated docs as a collapsible **Revision History** section
- Added ReSpec-native *This version* / *Latest published version* header links pointing into the versioned Pages layout
- Fixed markdown rendering: removed a custom client-side renderer that ran after ReSpec and flattened its output — markdown is now rendered natively by ReSpec
- Fixed the visible copyright (CC BY 4.0) that was being suppressed by an `!important` CSS rule
- Fixed the `references` object property being pulled into ReSpec's auto-generated References section: its anchor collided with the reserved `references` section id, so entity anchors matching reserved ReSpec ids are now prefixed `lomo-`
- Cleared all ReSpec validation errors and warnings: removed the invalid W3C `group`/`license` config (this is not a W3C document), added the Conformance section, slugified the thematic class-group anchors, and neutralised the literal `{{ }}` in this changelog that ReSpec's cross-reference parser rejected
- Fixed class-diagram boxes overflowing in Firefox (#128): node boxes were sized from an assumed 7px average character width, which happened to fit Chromium's text metrics but was too narrow for Firefox's, so the property lists spilled outside the box. Node dimensions are now measured on a canvas using the same font Cytoscape renders with, wrapping is mirrored so the line count matches what is drawn, and the rendered `font-size`/`font-weight`/`font-family`/`line-height`/`text-max-width` are driven from the same constants as the measurement so the two cannot drift apart
- Added a **Namespaces** section, immediately before Classes, listing the prefix bindings the document uses (#126). The table is driven by the namespaces actually rendered rather than by every binding rdflib knows about, so it stays free of the dozens of prefixes rdflib pre-binds but LOMO never mentions
- Replaced full IRIs with CURIEs throughout the class and property reference (#126): the `has domain` / `has range` / `sub-property of` / `inverse of` entries and the Range column of the per-class property tables previously fell back to the raw IRI whenever a term carried no `rdfs:label`, which is always the case for external vocabularies since they are not loaded into the graph. `http://www.w3.org/ns/dcat#Resource` now reads `dcat:Resource`. Each entity keeps its full IRI, with the CURIE shown beside it
- Fixed unreadable text in dark mode (#125): the stylesheet's class-level rules override ReSpec's dark-mode defaults, so hard-coded light backgrounds and dark foregrounds were rendering light-on-light or dark-on-dark. Added dark-mode counterparts for the class/property definitions, Usage Note, Example and Turtle Instantiation blocks (dark tints keeping the existing colour coding), the See also blocks, the collapsible summaries, the IRI/type code chips, the property tables, and the citation, back-reference and diagram-legend text
- Fixed the Range column of each class's property table (#136): the table was assembled from five separately aggregated lists zipped by position, which paired properties with other properties' ranges and printed union ranges as blank-node ids. Each property now carries its own row (`sparql/classes.rq`), union ranges are left out as in the Properties section, and a test checks every row against the declared `rdfs:range`
- Added Scope Rule and Institutional Rule to the Rules view of the class diagram (#137): the diagram's class-to-group map did not list them, so they fell into "other" and the filtered Rules diagram left them out while the full diagram showed them. A test checks every LOMO class has a diagram group
- Fixed the light/dark/auto mode toggle showing the opposite theme on load: the page declared no `color-scheme`, so ReSpec disabled its dark stylesheet before it loaded and the toggle then inverted it (a light OS got a black page with unreadable tables, a dark OS a white page with light-blue text). The page now declares `light dark`, which also lets code blocks follow auto mode. Also: Namespaces row headers no longer look like column headers in dark mode, and the IRI chip meets AA contrast in light mode
- Fixed references missing from the References section (#147): it collected `rdfs:seeAlso` from classes only, so the two DOIs on the ontology itself (Palmirani et al. 2025, Corazza et al. 2026) never appeared; it now reads the ontology, its classes and its properties. The five EU Vocabularies links (on `hasRequirementType`, `involvesProcess`, `withRole`) have labels, so See also and the Controlled Vocabularies table show their titles instead of long portal URLs. Plain-text references escape `&` and `<`
- Redesigned the class diagram (#146): classes sit in labelled Rules, Actions & Results, Agents and Temporal boxes; a property whose range is its own class is marked ↻ in the class box and counted in a badge, instead of a fan of loops (Temporal Specification had twelve); classes show their full labels; hovering a class highlights its relations; the diagram follows the light/dark mode; and a Full screen button gives it the whole display. Filtering by group no longer re-runs the layout, so the arrangement is kept. The layout picker is gone: the committed `diagram-layout.json` is the default, now a compact arrangement that reads from rules to actions, agents and results; editors can still drag classes (or whole groups) and export the positions from *Layout tools*

#### Ontology Fixes

- Fixed typo: "synctactic" → "syntactic" in `Definition` scope note (3 occurrences)
- Removed `lomo:isSemanticDefinition` from `Definition` usage examples
- Fixed `lomo:addresser`/`lomo:addressee` in `vann:usageExample` for `:hasAgentRole` and `:forAgent` — replaced with correct OP AT Role URIs
- Fixed invalid Turtle in `:involvesProcess` `vann:usageExample`: wrapped bare URIs in `< >` and used the concept IRI `<http://data.europa.eu/9bx/1>` instead of the resolver URL (#114)
- Harmonised annotation predicate on the temporal datatype properties (`:instant`, `:duration`, `:repetitions`, `:temporalLabel`, `:frequency`): `vann:usageNote` → `vann:usageExample`, matching the rest of the ontology (#119)
- Corrected `skos:scopeNote` errors across several entities (#120): removed references to undeclared `hasCondition`/`usesDefinition` (`PrescriptiveRule`), `hasReferent` (`Definition`) and the undeclared `TemporalRule` class (`ScopeRule`); fixed the `PrescriptiveRule` property list numbering (3 → 5 gap) and aligned its `hasRequirementType` categories with the authority tables; fixed the `isDefinedBy` typo "fined" → "defined"; corrected the `LegalRule` claim about `establishedIn` (ELI legal resources, not only `eli:Work`); noted the narrower `Observation` concept on `hasCategory`; and used full temporal-function URIs instead of the `:` prefix in the `atTime` inline patterns
- Fixed `dcterms:Software` → `dctype:Software` (DCMI Type Vocabulary): `dcterms:Software` is undefined in the DCTERMS namespace. Added `@prefix dctype:` and corrected the `:references` scope note, the `:hasCapability` `rdfs:domain`, and two `ex:Software_*` example types. `dcterms:Standard` is a valid DCTERMS class and was left unchanged (#120)
- Fixed broken `vann:usageExample`/`skos:example` snippets (#121): replaced the undeclared `lrmo:` namespace with `ex:` in the `PrescriptiveRule` example; replaced undeclared `lomo:establishedInResource` with `rdfs:seeAlso` (and repaired the blank-node termination) in the `Definition`/`equivalentDefinition` examples; corrected the copy-pasted `isDefinedBy` comment; split the malformed two-in-one `ActionResult` `skos:example` into two; renumbered the duplicate "Example 3" → "Example 4" in `specifies`; changed the second relationship `rdfs:comment` to `skos:note` in `derogatesFrom`; and noted that the abbreviated `PrescriptiveRule` instances in the relationship examples omit the mandatory `lomo:produces`
- Fixed small typos and stale wording (#153): `TemporalSpecification`'s scope note now lists the trigger function; `hasResultCategory` and the result category and temporal function scheme descriptions name concepts by their Title Case labels (Report, Deadline, …); "a event" → "an event" in Trigger; a stray " ;" inside a Right example and a trailing space in the Right comment are removed; the `withRole` scope note no longer says "In the context of LOMO 1.0.0"
- Aligned the Prescriptive Rule shape with the ontology (#161): a prescriptive rule has at least one deontic modality. The ontology already required it (`owl:minCardinality 1`) but the shape allowed none and at most one; it now requires at least one and sets no maximum. The open TODO on the axiom is resolved

#### Annotations

- Added `skos:scopeNote`, `skos:example` (×2), and `vann:usageExample` to the `:Action` class, which previously had only label/comment (#115)
- Added `vann:usageExample` to the rule subclasses `:SubstantiveRule`, `:ImplementationAndOversightRule`, `:ConstitutiveRule`, `:InstitutionalRule`, `:ScopeRule`, and to `:TemporalSpecification`; added the missing `skos:example` to `:TemporalSpecification` and `:Definition` (#116)
- Added `skos:scopeNote`, `skos:example`, and `vann:usageExample` to the previously bare properties `:after`, `:before`, `:immediatelyAfter`, `:immediatelyBefore`, `:in`, `:hasDeonticModality`, `:hasResultCategory`, `:defines`, `:hasDefiniendum`, `:hasDefiniens` (#117)
- Completed the partial annotations on `:hasRequirementType`, `:withRole`, `:hasNext`, `:hasPrevious`, `:equivalentDefinition`, `:references`, `:relatesTo`, `:isDefinedBy`, `:legalText`, `:termLabel` (added the missing `skos:scopeNote`/`skos:example`/`vann:usageExample`); also repaired the incomplete-fragment `vann:usageExample` snippets on `:atTime`, `:hasNext`, `:hasPrevious`, `:duration`, `:repetitions`, `:temporalLabel`, `:frequency` so that every usageExample in the ontology is now valid standalone Turtle (#118)
- Added a `skos:note` (the "Design Overview" narrative) to the ontology, rendered as the Design Overview section of the generated docs; removed `dcterms:abstract` (superseded by it)
- Added `skos:scopeNote`, `skos:example` and `vann:usageExample` to the two union classes `AgentOrType` and `ActionResultOrType` (#162), the only classes without them: they explain that individuals are typed with the specific class or the type, and that the union exists as the range of `forAgent` / `hasResult` / `isComposedOf` so those properties accept either

#### Controlled Vocabularies

- Changed 'rdfs:comment' to 'skos:definition' for concepts
- Gave every concept and concept scheme in the four vocabularies one `skos:notation` (#140): a lower-case `snake_case` code, unique across the schemes, that stays fixed when a label is reworded or translated. The digital-dimension and deontic codes are the ones AI4DRPM already stores (`data`, `observation`, `digital_public_service`, `digital_solution`, `obligation`, `permission`, `prohibition`, `right`); result category and temporal function get `report`, `certificate`, `deadline`, `validity`, `starting_point`, `trigger`; the schemes get `digital_dimension`, `deontic_modality`, `result_category`, `temporal_function`
- The vocabulary shapes require exactly one notation per concept and scheme, and a test checks the codes are unique across the four files (#140)
- Added two digital dimensions (#141): **Process Automation** (`process_automation`), a top concept for procedures carried out by a system rather than manually, and **Data Flow** (`data_flow`), narrower than Data, for requirements that move data between parties (reporting, notification, transmission). Both had been added locally by AI4DRPM; the scheme now has four top concepts
- Sharpened the definitions of Data, Digital Public Service and Digital Solution (#142) to the wording AI4DRPM classifies with, naming what falls inside each and where they meet: interoperability in a public-service context is Digital Public Service; governance mechanisms and regulatory sandboxes are not Digital Solutions. Data keeps its reporting wording as the broader concept, and its scope note sends requirements that move data between parties to Data Flow
- Made every English label singular and Title Case, and named the schemes by what they are (#143): Digital Solutions → Digital Solution; report, certificate, deadline, validity, starting point, trigger → Report, Certificate, Deadline, Validity, Starting Point, Trigger; the schemes are Digital Dimensions (was LFDS Digital Dimensions / Digital Dimensions Scheme), Deontic Modality, Result Category and Temporal Function, with `skos:prefLabel` and `rdfs:label` equal. IRIs do not change; the LFDS origin stays in `dcterms:source`
- Added French labels to every concept and concept scheme (#144): one `skos:prefLabel` per language, `@en` and `@fr`, French capitalised on the first word only (e.g. Service public numérique, Solution numérique, Interdiction, Droit, Échéance, Élément déclencheur). Definitions and scope notes stay English. The vocabulary shapes require one English and one French label, so a concept cannot ship untranslated. The rdf-to-table requirements query reads the English label only, so the exported categories are not doubled with their French names
- Updated the texts that still described three digital dimensions (#151): ontology use case 7, the `hasCategory` scope note (which also said *Digital Solutions*) and the Digital Dimensions scheme's comment and description now name Process Automation, and Observation and Data Flow as narrower concepts of Data
- Stated `skos:topConceptOf` on the ten top concepts of the deontic modality, result category and temporal function schemes (#152), which listed them only through the scheme's `skos:hasTopConcept`; a test checks that top-concept and broader/narrower links are stated both ways in all four schemes
- Every concept scheme now carries the release metadata of the ontology it ships with (#154): `owl:versionInfo`, `dcterms:modified`, `dcterms:creator` and `dcterms:license` (CC BY 4.0). Previously only Deontic Modality had a version, mis-indented. A test keeps the schemes' version, date and licence equal to the ontology's, so a release bumps all five files together
- Observation states its correspondence to the RDF Data Cube as a mapping, `skos:closeMatch qb:Observation` (#160), not only in its scope note
- The vocabulary shapes no longer list concepts or fix top-concept counts by hand (#159): each concept shape selects every concept `skos:inScheme` its scheme (a SHACL-AF `sh:SPARQLTarget`, which the EU testbed validator supports), and each scheme requires at least one top concept instead of an exact number. A new concept is validated without editing the shapes

#### Maintenance

- Changed the release date to 2026-10-21
- Bumped the ontology and the four concept schemes to version `1.1.0` (`owl:versionInfo`, the ontology's `owl:versionIRI`) and set `dcterms:modified` to `2026-09-28` (#148); 1.1.0 rather than a 1.0.1 patch, since the release adds concepts and vocabulary constraints
- Bumped `pyproject.toml` version to `1.1.0`
- Renamed `scripts/run_queries.py` → `scripts/generate_docs.py` (the script generates the documentation site, not just runs queries)
- Configured GitLab Pages to publish every release version to its own subdirectory (`/X.Y.Z/`), plus a `/latest/` alias and a root redirect; each version's docs are rendered from that tag's ontology by the current tooling
- Corrected the malformed release tag `v.0.6.1` → `v0.6.1`
- Removed the now-dead `vann:usageNote` fallback from `sparql/datatype_properties.rq` (no property uses `vann:usageNote` after the harmonisation above)
- Fixed the concept namespaces the table/RDF scripts use (#145): `table-to-rdf` minted `http://data.europa.eu/leg/{digital-dimensions,deontic-modality,temporal-function}#…` IRIs, and the `rdf-to-table` data-flows query read temporal functions under `#`, while the vocabularies define `…/Data`, `…/Obligation`, `…/deadline`. Generated data now joins with the vocabularies' labels, definitions and hierarchy
- Pages publishes every release from v1.0.0 on at its own `/X.Y.Z/` (#149): the site build kept only the newest tag of each major version, so tagging a release would have removed the previous one and broken its *This version* link. Tags before v1.0.0 stay unpublished. The tag rules of the `pages` and `shacl_data` jobs now match any major version, not only `v1.`
- Removed five rdf-to-table report scripts that no longer ran (#158): `lomo-requirements.py`, `lomo-data.py`, `lomo-data-flows.py`, `lomo-digital-solutions.py` and `lomo-dps.py` read a data file that is no longer in the repository and unpacked fewer columns than their queries now return. `generate-tables.py` produces the same reports from the same queries; `lomo-assessment.py` stays
- The table-to-RDF converter maps LFDS category cells to the digital dimensions they name (#157): "Data flow" goes to Data Flow instead of Data, "process digitalisation (and) automation" to Process Automation, matching ignores case, and a cell naming several dimensions ("Data, Digital Solution", "… / Data", "… and …") yields each of them; before, such cells became a single example.org category. Unknown categories still fall back to example.org
- Housekeeping (#163): the class property tables no longer repeat one HTML `id` seven times (the id was unused); `requirements.txt` is re-saved as UTF-8 (it was UTF-16) with an accurate header noting that `scripts/download-lfds` also needs `tulit`; `docs_wip/` is no longer tracked, as commit acff444 intended, and is ignored
- Instance data uses only concepts of the controlled vocabularies (#156), and the data shapes enforce it: `hasCategory` values must be Digital Dimensions concepts and `hasResultCategory` values Result Category concepts. In 30 LFDS acts, the 195 example.org categories are replaced by the dimensions their source cells name, using the #157 mapping (mostly cells naming several dimensions, e.g. "Data, Digital Solution"); parts naming no dimension (e.g. "Emergency response", "EU portal", prose misplaced in the category column) are dropped, and the converter now drops them with a warning instead of minting example.org categories. In the batch_1 examples, `example:Report` becomes Report and the 25 other example result categories are removed; csa2's `lomo:Cooperation` becomes Data Flow; the misspelt `administative-obligation` requirement type is corrected. No concept is added to the vocabularies

